  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Additional showers and thunderstorms through\nthis evening could lead to further instances of flash flooding.\n\nThe stalled frontal boundary draped over the area continues to\nremain in place. A broad area of low pressure will develop along\nthis boundary later today and shift east to the south of Long\nIsland. As this does so, additional shower and thunderstorm\ndevelopment is expected through the late afternoon hours. In\nfact, a remnant outflow boundary from this morning's convection\ncurrently lies over southern New Jersey and into the northern\nDelmarva which is the primary focus for additional convection \nthrough the afternoon/evening. As noted previously, the best\nconvergence and dynamics is now located to our north and east,\nso aside for locally heavy rains in the areas mentioned above,\nthe worst of the significant flash flood risk is now beyond us. \n\nAs a result, have opted to maintain the Flood Watch through 8 PM\nthis evening as several Flash Flood Warnings have been issued\nearlier today. With PWATs remaining on the higher side, rainfall\nrates in excess of 2 inches per hour will be possible. The\nthreat for severe weather remains low, however portions of the\nDelmarva remain in a MARGINAL risk for severe weather as those\nareas remain south of the stalled boundary. Damaging wind gusts\nwill be the primary hazard in any severe storm.\n\nOtherwise, as we head into tonight, low pressure will continue\nto move away, resulting in a gradually diminishing of activity.\nStill some residual showers may reside overnight, before\nbecoming more isolated on Tuesday. Some additional localized\nflooding is possible on Tuesday, but overall the threat remains\nminimal.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...High pressure builds in for Wednesday with the \nchance of showers and storms returning again for Thursday and \nFriday. \n\nHigh pressure builds in for Wednesday with seasonable\ntemperatures and partly to mostly sunny skies expected. \n\nAs we head towards the end of the week into next weekend, a new \nfrontal boundary and associated upper level trough will move in from \nthe west bringing a return to chances of showers and storms. POPs \nincrease to 40-60 percent by Thursday evening and continue \nthrough Friday. It's possible that the front and associated \nshowers/storms linger for the start of next weekend however \nforecast confidence is low by this time.",
